    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Which Portuguese Sea Words Will You Hear on a Boat in Madeira?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>27 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Mostly words about the mood of the sea and the shape of the coast: mar (sea), ondulação (swell), mar chão (a flat sea), baía (bay), fajã (flat land at the foot of a cliff), golfinho (dolphin) and lobo-marinho (monk seal). Many of them are also hiding inside the place names between Funchal and Ponta do Sol.</p>

      <h2>Why learn sea words rather than restaurant phrases?</h2><p>Because on this coast the map is written in them. Ponta do Sol is the point of the sun. Câmara de Lobos takes its name from the lobo-marinho, the monk seal. Ribeira Brava is the wild stream. Fajã dos Padres is the priests' fajã. Once you know a dozen words, the names you pass stop being sounds and start describing what you are looking at.</p><p>The second reason is practical. Sea words are the ones people use about your day: whether the swell is up, whether the water is flat at the swim stop, whether that fin was a dolphin. Menu Portuguese you can point your way through. The sea you cannot point at.</p><h2>How do you say Portuguese words without guessing?</h2><p>Learn five sounds and most of the words below fall into place. European Portuguese, the kind spoken in Madeira, sounds quite different from the Brazilian Portuguese of most language apps, mainly because it swallows its unstressed vowels.</p><ol><li><strong>Final s sounds like sh.</strong> Lobos is LOH-boosh, Padres is PAH-dresh.</li><li><strong>Final o sounds like oo</strong>, and is short: mergulho is mer-GOO-lyoo.</li><li><strong>ão is a nasal ow</strong>, said through the nose, close to the end of "town" with a cold.</li><li><strong>lh is like the lli in million</strong>, and <strong>nh is like the ny in canyon</strong>.</li><li><strong>j, and g before e or i, sound like the s in measure</strong>: fajã is fah-ZHÃ, Girão is zhee-RÃW.</li></ol><p>The hints in the tables are rough English spellings. Capitals show the stressed syllable.</p><h2>Which words describe the state of the sea?</h2><p>These are the ones you hear first, often on the pontoon before anyone has stepped aboard. They decide whether the morning is a swim morning.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Say it roughly</th><th>What it means out here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>mar</td><td>mahr</td><td>The sea, and also its condition: "o mar está bom" means the sea is good today.</td></tr><tr><td>onda</td><td>ON-dah</td><td>A single wave, the kind that breaks on the pebbles.</td></tr><tr><td>ondulação</td><td>on-doo-lah-SÃW</td><td>Swell: long, rolling waves sent from distant Atlantic storms. They can roll in under a windless sky, which is why swell heads every sea forecast.</td></tr><tr><td>mar chão</td><td>mahr SHÃW</td><td>A calm sea with only a weak swell. Chão is also the word for the floor, and that is the picture: water you could almost walk on.</td></tr><tr><td>mar picado</td><td>mahr pee-KAH-doo</td><td>Choppy water: short, steep waves raised by local wind.</td></tr><tr><td>mar bravo</td><td>mahr BRAH-voo</td><td>A rough, angry sea. On a small boat it usually means another day.</td></tr><tr><td>vento</td><td>VEN-too</td><td>Wind.</td></tr><tr><td>maré</td><td>mah-REH</td><td>Tide.</td></tr><tr><td>leste</td><td>LESHT</td><td>The east wind, and in Madeira something more: tempo de leste is the hot, dry weather that blows in from Africa, often with a haze of dust.</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Which coast words are hidden in the place names?</h2><p>Almost every name on the south coast is a description. Read these and a road map of Madeira suddenly makes sense.</p><ul><li><strong>baía</strong> (bah-EE-ah): bay. The bay of Funchal is the Baía do Funchal.</li><li><strong>enseada</strong> (en-see-AH-dah): a cove, smaller and more closed than a bay.</li><li><strong>ponta</strong> (PON-tah): a point or headland. Ponta do Sol, Ponta da Cruz and Ponta do Garajau all stick out into the sea.</li><li><strong>cabo</strong> (KAH-boo): a cape, a bigger headland. Cabo Girão is the famous one.</li><li><strong>fajã</strong> (fah-ZHÃ): a flat piece of land at the foot of a cliff, built up by old rockfalls or lava. Our page on <a href="/posts/what-is-a-faja-in-madeira">what a fajã is</a> explains why people farm them.</li><li><strong>calhau</strong> (kah-LYOW): a pebble or boulder, and in Madeira the stony shore itself.</li><li><strong>ilhéu</strong> (ee-LYEH-oo): an islet, a rock too big to be a rock and too small to be an island.</li><li><strong>ribeira</strong> (ree-BAY-rah): a stream and the valley it cuts. Ribeira Brava sits at the mouth of one.</li></ul><h2>Which animal names should you listen for?</h2><p>These are the words that make everyone turn their head at once. Nothing on this list is ever promised on a trip, but the names are worth knowing in case.</p><ul><li><strong>golfinho</strong> (gol-FEE-nyoo): dolphin.</li><li><strong>baleia</strong> (bah-LAY-ah): whale. A sperm whale has its own name, <strong>cachalote</strong> (kah-shah-LOT).</li><li><strong>lobo-marinho</strong> (LOH-boo mah-REE-nyoo): the Mediterranean monk seal, literally sea wolf. It is the wolf in <a href="/posts/why-is-camara-de-lobos-called-chamber-of-wolves">the name of Câmara de Lobos</a>.</li><li><strong>tartaruga</strong> (tar-tah-ROO-gah): turtle.</li><li><strong>garajau</strong> (gah-rah-ZHOW): a tern, the small white seabird that dives head first.</li><li><strong>cagarra</strong> (kah-GAH-rah): Cory's shearwater, the big brown and white seabird that glides low over the waves.</li><li><strong>peixe-voador</strong> (PAY-sh voo-ah-DOR): flying fish.</li></ul><h2>Which words are about the boat itself?</h2><p>A short set, and useful because it tells you where to stand and where to sit.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Word</th><th>Say it roughly</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>barco</td><td>BAR-koo</td><td>Boat, any size.</td></tr><tr><td>proa</td><td>PRO-ah</td><td>The bow, the front.</td></tr><tr><td>popa</td><td>POH-pah</td><td>The stern, the back.</td></tr><tr><td>bombordo / estibordo</td><td>bom-BOR-doo / esh-tee-BOR-doo</td><td>Port and starboard: left and right when you face the bow.</td></tr><tr><td>pontão</td><td>pon-TÃW</td><td>Pontoon, the floating walkway in the marina.</td></tr><tr><td>cais</td><td>kysh</td><td>Quay, the fixed stone edge of a harbour.</td></tr><tr><td>colete salva-vidas</td><td>koo-LET SAL-vah VEE-dash</td><td>Life jacket.</td></tr><tr><td>enjoo</td><td>en-ZHOH-oo</td><td>Seasickness.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Three verbs complete the set at the swim stop: <strong>nadar</strong> (nah-DAR), to swim, <strong>saltar</strong> (sal-TAR), to jump, and <strong>mergulhar</strong> (mer-goo-LYAR), to dive or duck under. Those three are what Fajã dos Padres and Ribeira Brava are for on the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Chifbay Day Trip from Funchal</a>.</p><h2>Do you need any Portuguese on a boat trip?</h2><p>No, and it is worth saying plainly. Nobody needs a word of Portuguese to enjoy two or three hours on the water, and if you came to Madeira to switch off, skip this page with a clear conscience. The words are for people who like to know what things are called.</p><p>Where they help most is on the coast itself. Ask what a place is called, then ask what the name means, and the answer often explains the landscape: why a point is called after the sun, why a bay is called after seals. On a private boat that kind of question is easy, because the whole boat is yours and Chifbay's skippers are local. Our <a href="/private-boat-tour-madeira">private boat tour in Madeira</a> page has the practical side, and the <a href="/posts/portuguese-phrases-for-madeira-visitors">everyday Portuguese phrases guide</a> covers greetings, ordering and thank you.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>How do you say thank you in Portuguese on the boat?</h3><p>A man says obrigado and a woman says obrigada, roughly oh-bree-GAH-doo and oh-bree-GAH-dah.</p><h3>What is the Portuguese word for a calm sea?</h3><p>Mar calmo is the plain phrase. Mar chão, used in Portugal, means a calm sea with only a weak swell.</p><h3>Is a lobo-marinho a sea lion?</h3><p>No. In Portugal lobo-marinho means the Mediterranean monk seal, Monachus monachus. In Madeira it lives mainly around the Desertas Islands.</p><h3>How do you pronounce Funchal?</h3><p>Roughly foon-SHAHL, with the stress on the last syllable.</p>

      <blockquote>Learn mar chão before obrigado, and you will know what kind of morning it is.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

